-- `@[trusted]` rescues an otherwise-undischargeable `cap verified` obligation
-- (§`@[trusted] — a scoped, loud escape hatch`, 2026-07-30), without dropping
-- `cap verified` for the whole module.
--
-- `inner` demands `len(_) > 0` on its argument. `go` calls it on a bare
-- `List(Int)` parameter with no guard establishing that fact, so the
-- obligation is SKIPPED (solver-undecided). Under `cap verified` that skip
-- is ordinarily escalated to a compile error -- see the discriminating
-- companion `reject/t131_refine_let_annotation_false` for what an
-- undischarged obligation looks like when nothing rescues it. Here `go` is
-- annotated `@[trusted]`, so the escalation is suppressed and the obligation
-- is recorded as `Trusted` instead: an assertion, not a proof, but not a
-- compile error either.
--
-- `not_ok` (deliberately absent from this file) would still error without
-- `@[trusted]` -- see `reject/t133_refine_trusted_violation_not_rescued` for
-- the load-bearing limit (a definite VIOLATION is never rescued), and the
-- `trusted` suite in `test/test_refinecheck.ml` for the sibling-scoping case
-- (a `cap verified` module with one `@[trusted]` function and one ordinary
-- one: only the ordinary one still errors).
--
-- See specs/lang/refinement-types.md, "`@[trusted]` — a scoped, loud escape
-- hatch".
mod T132 do

  cap verified

  fn inner(xs : {List(Int) | len(_) > 0}) : Int do
    0
  end

  @[trusted]
  fn go(zs : List(Int)) : Int do
    inner(zs)
  end

  fn main() : Int do
    go([1])
  end

end
